From c3fe901ba8365451fa3357a0d050dfd37aeb8eb5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adrien Delmas <adrien@adriendelmas.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 10:40:11 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 132/141] hydra: anisotropic roughness on conductor and
 generalized Schlick

MaterialX gives a metal's roughness as two alphas along the UV tangent and
bitangent; the mapping averaged them and a brushed metal rendered round.
Cycles' metallic BSDF stretches along its tangent only, with an anisotropy
in [0, 1] and a rotation: the alphas become alpha = sqrt(ax ay),
A = (1 - min/max) / 0.9 and a quarter turn when the bitangent's alpha is
larger - for a value at translation, for a wire as MaterialX nodes - and an
unwired tangent is MaterialX's UV tangent (K-91).

A wired BSDF weight is now emptied rather than erased on the node from
Hydra, so the original wire does not show through the overlay.
